  "text": "The Graph tab draws one object's neighbourhood. It answers “what is this connected to?” and “how are these two connected?”. Start from any object and choose Steps out: 1 (its direct links), 2 or 3. Click an object to select it. Its links and neighbours stay lit, the rest fades, and the panel lists its relationships named from its own side: a customer has orders, an order is placed by a customer and has lines. Double-click (or Expand) grows the graph from that object. A dashed border means it has not been expanded yet. New objects appear beside the one they came from; what was already drawn stays where it is. Shift-click a second object for the shortest path between them, read in order: Canyon Hammock → ordered in → Order 1271 → placed by → Ava Byrne → orders → Order 1344 → contains → Coastal Gloves. Every link among the objects shown is drawn, whichever object was expanded: an order line and its product, both on screen, are joined. A more badge means an object has more links of some kind than the 8 drawn; the line above the graph names them. Open the object to page through all of them. Hide removes an object and whatever only it connected; Start here redraws from it. All labels names every link at once. The panel beside the graph shows the selected object; with nothing selected it says how to read the graph. The wheel scrolls the page; Ctrl + scroll, or a pinch, zooms; drag the background to move. A graph stops at 150 objects and says so. A link that could not be followed (a table you cannot read, a hidden key) is named above the graph, not silently dropped. Which suppliers does Ava Byrne depend on? Start from Ava Byrne and choose 3 steps out: her orders, their products and those products' suppliers appear. Click a supplier, then Shift-click Ava, for the chain from one to the other."
